&reftitle.description;
stringfalsestream_socket_recvfrom resourcesocket intlength intflags0 stringnulladdress&null;
stream_socket_recvfrom receives up to length bytes of data from a socket.

&reftitle.changelog;





    &Version;
    &Description;





    8.0.0

     Now throws a ValueError if length is less than 1.
     Previously, an E_WARNING was raised and &false; was returned.

  &reftitle.examples;



    stream_socket_recvfrom example


<?php

// Open a server socket on a free port of localhost
$server = stream_socket_server('tcp://127.0.0.1:0');
$client = stream_socket_client('tcp://' . stream_socket_get_name($server, false));

// Accept the connection
$socket = stream_socket_accept($server);

// Send some normal data, then one byte of out-of-band data
fwrite($client, 'hello');
stream_socket_sendto($client, '!', STREAM_OOB);

// Grab a packet (1500 is a typical MTU size) of OOB data
echo "Received Out-Of-Band: '" . stream_socket_recvfrom($socket, 1500, STREAM_OOB) . "'\n";

// Take a peek at the normal in-band data, but don't consume it
echo "Data: '" . stream_socket_recvfrom($socket, 1500, STREAM_PEEK) . "'\n";

// Get the exact same packet again, but remove it from the buffer this time
echo "Data: '" . stream_socket_recvfrom($socket, 1500) . "'\n";

// Close it up
fclose($socket);
fclose($client);
fclose($server);


    &example.outputs;


Received Out-Of-Band: '!'
Data: 'hello'
Data: 'hello'

&reftitle.notes;
If a message received is longer than the length parameter, excess bytes may be discarded depending on the type of socket the message is received from (such as UDP).
stream_socket_recvfrom reads straight from the socket: it does not wait for data, so stream_set_timeout and default_socket_timeout do not apply and a blocking socket may block indefinitely. It also bypasses the stream buffer and the read filters, so data already read into the buffer by buffer-based stream functions (like fread or stream_get_line) is not seen, and a read filter such as string.toupper is not applied.
